WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.730
- I mean, how difficult was
it to keep working, though?

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.126
Like, 'cause it, I mean,

00:00:05.126 --> 00:00:07.980
I've heard, I have people in my life

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.030
that I know that have MS,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.680
but I've never experienced it personally,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.630
so I never like to pretend
I know what that's like.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.990
So, it's like, it has to be
wearing on you emotionally,

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:16.922
mentally, and physically.

00:00:16.922 --> 00:00:19.080
How was that to keep working?

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.000
- Well, there's four
different kinds of MS.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.150
So, not everyone is the same.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.530
Everyone's symptoms are different.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.720
Everyone's experience
with it is different.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.193
Every day is different.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.430
So that is just so that
people understand that.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.263
'Cause people are like,

00:00:33.263 --> 00:00:34.470
"Well, why aren't you like this one

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.700
and you're feeling better like this one?"

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.940
And it's like, everyone has a
different experience with it.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.310
So that's just a preface.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.800
Shooting the show was the
hardest thing I'd ever done

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.730
in my life, because I was
diagnosed during shooting.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.740
And I didn't know what
was happening to me.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.690
I couldn't walk.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.153
They had to use a
wheelchair to get me to set.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.947
I was freaking out until someone was like,

00:00:56.947 --> 00:00:57.810
"You need an MRI."

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:02.160
And then I found out on a
Monday after work that I had MS,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.480
you know, a disease that I'm gonna have

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.100
for the rest of my life.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.070
And then I started thinking
about the last four years

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.130
and I had very small symptoms.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.230
And I think that's important
for people, you know,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.510
let's not all be hypochondriacs
and run to the doctor

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.210
when our leg gets weak, but-

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.170
- But be aware.
- That was happening

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.640
to me four years ago,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.507
where we were on set and I'd go like,

00:01:22.507 --> 00:01:25.263
"Ooh, I think I'm tired," you know?

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.370
So, it presented itself
like a few years ago

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.700
until it just got as bad as it did.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.370
- That's hard, Christina, that's hard.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.170
Like getting that call and being at work

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:42.170
and having that kinda news
like laid on you and ooh.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.950
That's really hard.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.824
- Can I say it sucked balls?

00:01:45.824 --> 00:01:46.657
(audience laughing)

00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:47.850
- Yeah, you can
- Thank you.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.093
- Say whatever the hell
you want. (laughing)

